WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.367
Advanced RAG, part two.

2
00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:07.100
Ten more terms, from parsing messy documents to the long-context debate.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.833
Unstructured and LlamaParse are document parsing tools.

4
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:15.700
They take a messy PDF, with tables, columns, and scans,

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.233
run it through a parser,

6
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:21.020
and give you clean structured text, ready for chunking.

7
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:27.233
Parsing quality caps RAG quality, so legaltech and fintech job descriptions name these explicitly.

8
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:34.433
In agentic chunking, an LLM reads the document and decides where the chunks begin and end.

9
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:38.867
It groups by proposition or topic, rather than counting characters.

10
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:43.400
It is expensive, so use it where retrieval precision is worth the cost.

11
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:45.933
These two go together.

12
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:50.720
Groundedness means every claim can be traced back to the retrieved context.

13
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.933
It is the metric behind faithfulness.

14
00:00:53.233 --> 00:01:00.467
Citations make the model attribute each statement to its source chunk, and enterprise RAG treats them as table stakes.

15
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:04.133
Three ranking metrics for retrieval evals.

16
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:07.900
Hit rate asks: did the right document appear in the top k?

17
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.800
MRR asks: how high did the first right document rank?

18
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.967
And NDCG scores graded relevance across the whole list.

19
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:20.567
LanceDB is an embedded, serverless vector database.

20
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:28.100
It sits on a columnar format, a bit like DuckDB for vectors, so you get fast local RAG without running a server.

21
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.733
It joins the vector stores you learned earlier.

22
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:36.100
Elasticsearch, OpenSearch, and Vespa are the classic search engines.

23
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.133
All of them support vectors now.

24
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:45.933
So if a company already runs Elastic, its hybrid search often lives there, instead of in a dedicated vector database.

25
00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:50.367
This is the two-stage retrieval design in one line.

26
00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:54.206
A bi-encoder embeds the query and document separately.

27
00:01:54.206 --> 00:01:56.500
It is fast, so it powers retrieval.

28
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.099
A cross-encoder reads them together.

29
00:01:59.099 --> 00:02:02.100
It is slow but precise, so it powers reranking.

30
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:08.633
Here is more approximate nearest neighbor machinery, beyond HNSW and IVF.

31
00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:13.567
Product quantization compresses vectors into short codes, which saves memory.

32
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:18.967
Annoy, from Spotify, and ScaNN, from Google, are alternative libraries to FAISS.

33
00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:25.433
Multimodal RAG retrieves over images, tables, and diagrams, not just text.

34
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:27.867
Start with the figures in your documents.

35
00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:30.533
One route is CLIP-style shared embeddings.

36
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:35.167
The other is a vision language model that describes each visual as text.

37
00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:37.662
Either way, it ends up in the index.

38
00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:40.833
That is how you do RAG over manuals full of figures.

39
00:02:42.033 --> 00:02:45.600
The perennial debate: do million-token windows kill retrieval?

40
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.097
No.

41
00:02:46.097 --> 00:02:50.367
Cost, latency, and the lost in the middle problem keep RAG alive.

42
00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:55.467
The practical answer is both: retrieve, then give the model generous context.

43
00:02:56.667 --> 00:02:57.476
Let's recap.

44
00:02:57.476 --> 00:02:59.700
Parsing quality caps RAG quality.

45
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.100
Measure retrieval with hit rate, MRR, and NDCG, and check groundedness.

46
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.433
Bi-encoders retrieve and cross-encoders rerank.

47
00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:13.200
LanceDB, Elastic, and the ANN libraries are where it all lives.

48
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.100
And long context and RAG work best together.
